---
/**
 * EN home page — /
 *
 * Extended in Phase 4 with sober editorial prose, national-average DataCallout,
 * and quick-links into the map and key editorial pages.
 * Uses EditorialLayout (720px prose column + gated AdSlot).
 */
import EditorialLayout from '../layouts/EditorialLayout.astro';
import DataCallout from '../components/DataCallout.astro';
import { loadNationalAverage, loadIndex, loadCommune, latestCompleteYearRate } from '../lib/data.ts';

const nationalAvg = loadNationalAverage();

// Derive the reference year from any non-low-population commune
const index = loadIndex();
const sampleCut = index.find((c) => !c.low_population)?.cut ?? '13101';
const sampleCommune = loadCommune(sampleCut);
const referenceYear = sampleCommune.latestCompleteYear;
---

<EditorialLayout
  lang="en"
  title="Is Chile Safe — Chile Safety Map"
  description="Official CEAD crime statistics for every commune and region of Chile. Explore incidence rates, rankings, and trends by commune, region, or crime type."
  enPath="/"
  esPath="/es/"
>
  <section class="home-hero">
    <h1>Is Chile Safe? Official CEAD Crime Data for All 346 Communes</h1>
    <p class="home-intro">
      Chile Safety Map presents official crime-incidence data from Chile's Centro de Estudios
      y Análisis del Delito (CEAD) for all 346 communes and 16 regions of the country.
      Every figure you see on this site is sourced directly from that government dataset —
      no editorial weighting, no aggregated national "scores."
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What the Data Shows</h2>
    <p>
      The canonical metric on this site is the <strong>rate of reported offences per 100,000
      inhabitants</strong>. Using a rate rather than a raw count allows fair comparison
      between places of very different sizes: a commune with 10,000 residents and 50 reported
      offences in a year has a rate of 500 per 100,000 — the same relative frequency as a
      city of 500,000 with 2,500 reported offences.
    </p>
    <p>
      All rates are computed from CEAD's annual frequency tables, which aggregate police
      incident reports by commune, year, and crime family. According to CEAD data for
      {referenceYear}, the unweighted mean rate across non-low-population communes in Chile is
      shown below.
    </p>

    <DataCallout
      value={`${Math.round(nationalAvg).toLocaleString('en-US')} per 100k`}
      label={`National average reported crime rate — CEAD ${referenceYear}`}
      source="Source: CEAD (Centro de Estudios y Análisis del Delito)"
      locale="en"
    />

    <p>
      This figure represents the mean of individual commune rates, not a sum. Communes with
      fewer than 10,000 inhabitants are excluded from the national average calculation because
      small populations produce highly variable per-capita rates that would distort the
      comparison. Individual commune pages display rates regardless of population, with a
      clear note when population is below that threshold.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>How to Read Crime Rates</h2>
    <p>
      A higher rate means more reported offences per 100,000 residents — not necessarily that
      a place is objectively more or less liveable. Several factors affect the number:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Under-reporting:</strong> Not all incidents are reported to police.
        Under-reporting rates vary by crime type and are not uniform across communes or
        socioeconomic groups.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Floating population:</strong> Tourist areas and city centres may have lower
        resident populations relative to the number of people present on any given day,
        inflating per-resident rates.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Crime family composition:</strong> A high overall rate can reflect a high
        concentration of minor property offences rather than violent crime. Use the crime-family
        breakdown on each commune page to understand the composition.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Trend direction:</strong> A commune with a rate that has fallen steadily over
        several years may warrant a different reading than one at the same rate but trending
        upward. Each commune page shows a multi-year series.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      This site never assigns absolute "safe" or "dangerous" labels. It presents CEAD
      reported-incident data and lets you form your own informed view.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Explore the Data</h2>
    <p>
      Start with the interactive map to see all 346 communes coloured by incidence rate,
      or go directly to a commune, region, or crime type:
    </p>
    <ul class="home-links">
      <li><a href="/chile-crime-map/">Chile Crime Map — interactive choropleth</a></li>
      <li><a href="/is-chile-safe/">Is Chile Safe? — national overview FAQ</a></li>
      <li><a href="/is-santiago-safe/">Is Santiago Safe? — capital city data</a></li>
      <li><a href="/safest-cities-in-chile/">Communes with the lowest reported rates</a></li>
      <li><a href="/commune/santiago/">Santiago commune detail</a></li>
      <li><a href="/commune/valparaiso/">Valparaíso commune detail</a></li>
      <li><a href="/commune/antofagasta/">Antofagasta commune detail</a></li>
      <li><a href="/region/metropolitana/">Región Metropolitana</a></li>
      <li><a href="/crime/property/">Property crime national ranking</a></li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>About the CEAD Data Source</h2>
    <p>
      CEAD (Centro de Estudios y Análisis del Delito) is a unit of Chile's Subsecretaría de
      Prevención del Delito, which falls under the Ministry of the Interior and Public Security.
      It compiles police incident data from Carabineros de Chile and Policía de Investigaciones,
      and publishes annual frequency tables covering seven crime families:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Crimes against life and physical integrity</li>
      <li>Violent robbery and theft</li>
      <li>Intra-family violence</li>
      <li>Drug offences</li>
      <li>Arms offences</li>
      <li>Property crime</li>
      <li>Disorder and incivilities</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Data covers 2005 through the most recent complete year and is available at commune,
      regional, and national levels. Chile Safety Map fetches this data via CEAD's published
      endpoints, validates it against a strict schema (346 communes present, rates within
      plausible ranges), and stores the result as versioned static JSON in this repository.
      The <a href="/methodology/">Methodology page</a> documents the full pipeline, including
      population sources, rate calculations, and known limitations.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Limitations and Responsible Use</h2>
    <p>
      Crime statistics reflect <em>reported incidents</em>, not actual crime. Studies
      consistently show that property crime is under-reported at rates of 50–70 % in many
      countries; violent crime is somewhat better captured but still incomplete. CEAD data
      inherits these limitations and should be interpreted as an indicator of reporting
      patterns, not a comprehensive measure of public safety.
    </p>
    <p>
      Location decisions — whether to visit, live, or invest — involve factors beyond crime
      rates: economic opportunity, housing, healthcare, social networks, and personal
      circumstances. This site provides one data dimension to inform those decisions; it is
      not a substitute for on-the-ground research or local expertise.
    </p>
  </section>
</EditorialLayout>

<style>
  .home-hero {
    margin-bottom: var(--xl);
  }

  .home-intro {
    font-size: var(--text-body);
    color: var(--ink);
    line-height: 1.6;
    margin-top: var(--md);
  }

  .home-links {
    list-style: disc;
    padding-left: var(--lg);
    line-height: 2;
  }

  .home-links a {
    color: var(--primary);
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .home-links a:hover {
    text-decoration: underline;
  }

  ul {
    line-height: 1.8;
    padding-left: var(--lg);
  }

  p + p {
    margin-top: var(--md);
  }

  section {
    margin-bottom: var(--2xl);
  }
</style>
